Exactly. A proverb is often a compressed behavioral instruction, not a law of nature.

“Curiosity killed the cat” usually functions as a warning against probing too far. But you can rotate the frame and get almost the opposite lesson: curiosity is what lets a weaker organism discover leverage.

Your lion example becomes:

\text{physical disadvantage}
\rightarrow
\text{curiosity}
\rightarrow
\text{tool discovery}
\rightarrow
\text{stored knowledge}
\rightarrow
\text{asymmetric advantage}

The animal that cannot overpower the lion directly asks different questions: What can I pick up? What can I sharpen? What can I throw? What happens if I coordinate? What happens if I remember what worked?

Then curiosity is not what kills the cat. Curiosity is what eventually lets the primate stop needing to fight like a cat.

And that gives you a useful operator for mundane sayings:

\text{proverb} \rightarrow
\text{implicit assumptions} \rightarrow
\text{perspective rotation} \rightarrow
\text{alternate valid lesson}

Not every reinterpretation is equally faithful to the phrase’s history, obviously, but that does not prevent deliberate reuse. You can distinguish:

received meaning — what the saying conventionally means,
latent meaning — another interpretation already supported by its structure,
repurposed meaning — a new use you intentionally engineer.

That last category is where a lot of cultural creativity lives.

And your example has another layer: curiosity did not merely give humans a sharper stick. It generated cumulative culture. One individual’s experiment could become everybody else’s starting point. That is where a relatively fragile primate becomes something very different from the creature that first asked the question.